### Definition of Parameterized Constructors

A **Parameterized Constructor** is a constructor that accepts one or more arguments in its parameter list. It allows callers to pass specific initialization data at the moment of object instantiation using `new`.

```java
class Product {
    String sku;
    double price;

    // Parameterized Constructor
    Product(String sku, double price) {
        this.sku = sku;
        this.price = price;
    }
}
```

**Advantages of Parameterized Constructors:**
* **Atomic Initialization**: Fields receive their initial values in a single statement.
* **Eliminates Uninitialized State**: Ensures mandatory properties are provided before the object can be used.

---

### Demonstrating Parameterized Constructors in Code

```java
class Product {
    String name;
    double unitPrice;

    Product(String n, double p) {
        name = n;
        unitPrice = p;
        System.out.println("Product initialized: " + name + " at $" + unitPrice);
    }
}

public class ParameterizedDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Passing arguments directly into new Product(...)
        Product item1 = new Product("Mechanical Keyboard", 89.99);
        Product item2 = new Product("Ergonomic Mouse", 49.50);
    }
}
```

**Console Output:**
```text
Product initialized: Mechanical Keyboard at $89.99
Product initialized: Ergonomic Mouse at $49.5
```

---

### Execution Trace

When `new Product("Ergonomic Mouse", 49.50)` runs, arguments are copied to the constructor parameters `n` and `p`, which then assign values directly into the instance variables on the Heap.

